% ============================================================================
% valve-kind — the physiological CLASS each heart valve belongs to
% (atrioventricular vs. semilunar), decoded from adjectives already sitting
% unused inside `heart-valves.adj`'s own already-quoted NCI SEER sentences
% (adj-facts-stdlib, ANATOMY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `heart-valves.adj`
% (`valve_separates(valve, boundary)`, tricuspid/mitral/pulmonary/aortic →
% their own two-chamber-or-vessel boundary): that table's own header already
% quotes, verbatim, the SEER sentences that name each valve AND classify it
% as one of two kinds -- "atrioventricular" (the two valves between an atrium
% and its ventricle) or "semilunar" (the two valves at the base of a great
% vessel leaving a ventricle) -- a fact the valve/boundary schema had no room
% for:
%
%   tricuspid → atrioventricular
%     "The right atrioventricular valve is the tricuspid valve."
%
%   mitral → atrioventricular
%     "The left atrioventricular valve is the bicuspid, or mitral, valve."
%
%   pulmonary → semilunar
%     "The valve between the right ventricle and pulmonary trunk is the
%      pulmonary semilunar valve."
%
%   aortic → semilunar
%     "The valve between the left ventricle and the aorta is the aortic
%      semilunar valve."
%
% New `valve_kind(valve, kind)` table decodes that classifying adjective as
% each valve's own row. "Kind" (the valve's physiological CLASS) is
% categorically distinct from the parent table's "boundary" (the specific
% two chambers/vessels a valve sits between) -- the tricuspid's boundary is
% right_atrium_and_right_ventricle, but its kind is atrioventricular, a fact
% shared with the mitral valve even though their boundaries differ entirely.
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? valve_kind(tricuspid, $Kind)  % atrioventricular
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like the
% parent's own): each `row` lowers to a ground relation `valve_kind(valve,
% kind)` carrying the citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD
% binding query -- the engine returns the kind WITH its source, on the CPU,
% with zero model calls, and abstains on anything that is not one of the
% four valves (it never invents a kind). The query also runs BACKWARD --
% bind a kind and recall every valve of that kind:
%
%     ? valve_kind($V, semilunar)  % pulmonary, aortic
%
% A recall on `eustachian` -- a real cardiac valve name, but not one of the
% four valves this table (or its parent) covers -- correctly abstains rather
% than inventing a kind for it.
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME four spans already quoted
% inside `heart-valves.adj`'s own header, from the SAME NCI SEER Training
% Modules "Structure of the Heart" page that table already cites -- no new
% WebFetch, only a different schema decoding the classifying adjective of
% each already-verified quote. `trust authoritative` -- the same tier the
% parent table already carries (NCI is a primary U.S. government reference).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts, feedback_no_byte_arithmetic_for_llm.)
% ============================================================================

table valve_kind {
    columns valve, kind

    row (tricuspid, atrioventricular)
    row (mitral, atrioventricular)
    row (pulmonary, semilunar)
    row (aortic, semilunar)

    source "The right atrioventricular valve is the tricuspid valve."
    locator "https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/cardiovascular/heart/structure.html"
    trust authoritative
}
